Next we use the notation $\langle x_0, x_1, \dots, x_{n-1} \rangle$ for the function $\hat{p}$ where $p : \omega \to \mathbf{P}\omega$ is defined by:

$$p(i) = \begin{cases} x_i & \text{if } i < n; \\ \bot & \text{if } i \ge n. \end{cases}$$

$$(2.19) \quad \langle \rangle = \bot$$

$$(2.20) \quad \langle x \rangle = \lambda z.\, z \supset x, \bot$$

$$(2.21) \quad \langle x, y \rangle = \lambda z.\, z \supset x, (z - 1 \supset y, \bot)$$

$$(2.22) \quad \langle x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n \rangle = \lambda z.\, z \supset x_0, \langle x_1, \dots, x_n \rangle(z - 1).$$

Obviously we should formalize the subscript notation so that $u_x = u(x)$; then we find:

$$(2.23) \quad \langle x_0, x_1, \dots, x_n \rangle_i = \begin{cases} x_i & \text{if } i < n; \\ \bot & \text{if } i \ge n. \end{cases}$$

This gives us the method of LAMBDA-defining *finite sequences* (in a quite natural way), and the next step is to consider *infinite sequences*. But these are just the functions $\hat{p}$ where $p : \omega \to \mathbf{P}\omega$ is arbitrary. What we need then is a condition expressible in the language equivalent to saying $u = \hat{p}$ for some $p$. This is the same as

$$u = \lambda x.\, \bigcup \{ u_i \mid i \in x \}.$$

<!-- page 14 -->

but the $\cup$- and set-notation is not part of LAMBDA. We are forced into a recursive definition:

**Formalization (`sequences.lean`).** *Finite* tuples $\langle x_0,\ldots,x_{n-1}\rangle$ are graphs built from McCarthy conditional (`condSet`) and predecessor on arguments; *infinite* sequences are the spread $\lambda x.\,\bigcup\{u(i)\mid i\in x\}$, approximated by bounded unions `seqFunLt` and defined recursively via `seqShift`. Lean names below are the library entries.

**Definition (`seqCons`).** $\texttt{seqCons}(x,xs) = \mathbf{graph}(\lambda z.\, z \supset x,\, xs(\mathit{pred}\,z))$ — the graph of (2.22): head $x$, tail read from $xs$ off $z-1$.

**Theorem (`seqCons.body`).** The body $z \mapsto \mathit{condSet}(z,x,xs(\mathit{pred}\,z))$ is Scott-continuous.

*Proof.* `theorem_1_3.tuple` from continuity of conditional, identity, `funOf xs`, and `predSet`. ∎

**Definition (`seq2`).** $\texttt{seq2}(x,y) = \mathbf{graph}(\lambda z.\, z \supset x,\, (\mathit{pred}\,z \supset y,\,\bot))$ — model form of (2.21) for a pair.

**Theorem (`seq2.app`).** $\mathrm{funOf}(\texttt{seq2}\,x\,y)\,z = z \supset x,\, (\mathit{pred}\,z \supset y,\,\bot)$.

*Proof.* $(\beta)$ via `seq2.body_isScottContinuous`. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_2_22`).** Same as `seq2.app` for general `seqCons`: $\mathrm{funOf}(\texttt{seqCons}\,x\,xs)\,z = z \supset x,\, \mathrm{funOf}\,xs\,(\mathit{predSet}\,z)$.

*Proof.* `seqCons.body` and $(\beta)$. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_2_23_zero`, `seq2.zero`).** $\mathrm{funOf}(\texttt{seq2}\,x\,y)\,0 = x$ (argument $0 = \mathrm{ofNat}\,0$).

*Proof.* Unfold `seq2.app`; case split on conditional and predecessor. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_2_23_one`, `seq2.one`).** $\mathrm{funOf}(\texttt{seq2}\,x\,y)\,1 = y$.

*Proof.* Same. ∎

**Theorem (`eq_2_23_ge`).** For all $n$, $\mathrm{funOf}(\texttt{seq2}\,x\,y)\,(n+2) = \bot$ (empty beyond the pair).

*Proof.* `seq2.app` and arithmetic on `predSet` / `ofNat`. ∎

**Theorem (`seq2.tag_ne`).** $\texttt{seq2}(\mathrm{ofNat}\,0,x) \neq \texttt{seq2}(\mathrm{ofNat}\,1,y)$ (tags $0$ and $1$ are distinguishable).

*Proof.* Apply both sides to argument $0$; membership in $0$ vs $1$ contradicts. ∎

**Definition (`seqFun`).** $\texttt{seqFun}(u,x) = \bigcup \{ \mathrm{funOf}\,u\,(\mathrm{ofNat}\,i) \mid i \in x \}$ — the spread in (2.23) without $\lambda$-notation.

**Definition (`seqFunLt`).** $\texttt{seqFunLt}(u,x,n) = \bigcup \{ \mathrm{funOf}\,u\,(\mathrm{ofNat}\,i) \mid i \in x,\, i < n \}$ — the same union restricted to indices $< n$.

**Theorem (`mem_seqFunLt`).** $k \in \texttt{seqFunLt}\,u\,x\,n \leftrightarrow \exists i \in x,\, i < n \land k \in \mathrm{funOf}\,u\,(\mathrm{ofNat}\,i)$.

*Proof.* Unfold `seqFunLt` as a double indexed union. ∎

**Theorem (`seqFun.eq_iUnion_lt`).** $\texttt{seqFun}(u,z) = \bigcup_n \texttt{seqFunLt}(u,z,n)$.

*Proof.* Extensionality; each index $i$ contributes at stage $i+1$. ∎

**Theorem (`seqFun.left_isScottContinuous`, `seqFun.right_isScottContinuous`).** $u \mapsto \texttt{seqFun}(u,x)$ and $x \mapsto \texttt{seqFun}(u,x)$ are Scott-continuous.

*Proof.* Directed unions of Scott-continuous applications (`scottUnion`, `mem_scottUnion`, `funOf.isScottContinuous`). ∎

**Definition (`seqShift`).** $\texttt{seqShift}(u) = \mathbf{graph}(\lambda z.\, \texttt{seqFun}(u,\mathit{succSet}\,z))$ — reindex tail by successor on arguments (shift $u_i$ to $u_{i+1}$).

**Theorem (`seqShift.app`, `seqShift.ofNat`).** $\mathrm{funOf}(\texttt{seqShift}\,u)\,z = \texttt{seqFun}(u,\mathit{succSet}\,z)$; at numeric $i$, $\mathrm{funOf}(\texttt{seqShift}\,u)\,(\mathrm{ofNat}\,i) = \mathrm{funOf}\,u\,(\mathrm{ofNat}(i+1))$.

*Proof.* Graph $(\beta)$ and unfold `seqFun` / `succSet`. ∎

**Definition (`termShift`).** $\texttt{termShift}(u) = \mathbf{graph}(\lambda t.\, \mathrm{funOf}\,u\,(\mathit{succSet}\,t))$ — the same index shift on arbitrary arguments (for LAMBDA terms later).

**Theorem (`termShift.app`, `termShift.ofNat`).** As for `seqShift`, with `funOf` in place of `seqFun`.

*Proof.* `theorem_1_3` and $(\beta)$. ∎

**Theorem (`seqFunLt.zero`).** $\texttt{seqFunLt}\,u\,z\,0 = \bot$.

*Proof.* No index $i < 0$. ∎

**Theorem (`seqFunLt.succ`).** 
$$z \supset \mathrm{funOf}\,u\,0,\, \texttt{seqFunLt}(\texttt{seqShift}\,u,\mathit{predSet}\,z,n) = \texttt{seqFunLt}\,u\,z\,(n+1).$$
This is the recursive step Scott uses because $\cup$ is not primitive in LAMBDA.

*Proof.* Case split on McCarthy conditional; use `seqShift.ofNat` to align indices. ∎

**Theorem (`seqFunLt.term`).** Same identity with `termShift` in place of `seqShift` (term-level shift for definability proofs).

*Proof.* Parallel to `seqFunLt.succ`. ∎

*(Equations (2.19)–(2.20) for empty and singleton tuples are $\bot$ and `seqCons`-special cases; the dollar combinator and full LAMBDA term for `seqFun` continue in later notes.)*
